<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.18.7: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01185</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.18.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9e9cea0e-9246-4f90-ac4c-b86c8b71dfe9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of each specified term of the arithmetic sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as integers only. Do not include commas or periods.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Find the \(21^{st}\) term of a sequence where the first term is 3 and the common difference is 8. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>163</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Find the \(30^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is –14 and the common difference is 5. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>131</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> Find the \(16^{th}\) term of a sequence where the first term is 11 and the common difference is –6. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>–79</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of each specified term of the geometric sequences below. Hint: Enter your answers as integers only. Do not include commas or periods. </item>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Find the \(11^{th}\) term given the first term is 8 and the common ratio is 3. 
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>472392</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Find the \(10^{th}\) term given the first term is –6 and the common ratio is –2. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>3072</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-6, f_n=f_{n-1}-2\), for \(n≥2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n≥0\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_{n}=-2+(n-1)(-6) \) </item>
  <item>\(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \) </item>
  <item> \(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)+(-2)\) </item>
  <item> \(a_{n}=-6+(n)(-2)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_{n}=-6+(n-1)(-2) \)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=-20, f_n=f_{(n-1)}+5\) for \(n≥2\). Which of the following is the \(n^{th}\) term?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)+5\) </item>
  <item>\(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(-2)\) </item>
  <item> \(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\) </item>
  <item> \(a_{n}=5+(n-1)(-20)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_{n}=-20+(n-1)(5)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> A sequence is defined recursively by \(f_1=3\), \(f_n=2 \cdot f_{n-1}\), \(n\geq2\). Which of the following gives the \(n^{th}\) term for \(n\geq0\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\) </item>
  <item>\(f_n=3^n\) </item>
  <item> \(f_n=3+2n\) </item>
  <item> \(f_n=3+2^n\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f_n=3 \cdot 2^{n-1}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> A sequence of numbers is given by \(a_2=1\), \(a_3=5\), \(a_4=9\), \(a_5=13\), \(a_6=17\). Which expression can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(
    4n-3\) </item>
  <item> \(4n-7\) </item>
  <item> \(7n-4\) </item>
  <item> \(3n-4\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> b. \(4n-7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>A sequence of numbers is given by \(\frac{1}{27}\), \(\frac19\), \(\frac13\) 1, …. What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}-\frac{1}{3}(n-1)\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(\frac{1}{3})^{n-1}\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = \frac{1}{27}\div3(n-1)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_n = \frac{1}{27}(3)^{n-1}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>A sequence is given by the set of numbers 5, 1, -3, -7 … What is the equation that can be used to find the \(n^{th}\) term of the sequence? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_n = 5+(n-1)(-4)\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = 5(-4)^{n-1}\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = 5(4)^{n-1}\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n = 5-(n-1)(-4)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_n = 5 + (n-1)(-4)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>A sequence consists of the following terms: 1280, 320, 80, … What is the explicit formula for this sequence? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_n = 1280 - 960(n-1)\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n  = 1280 (4)^{n-1}\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n  = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\) </item>
  <item>\(a_n = 1280 \div 4(n-1)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_n  = 1280 (\frac 14)^{n-1}\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>An arithmetic sequence consists of the terms 1, 6, 11, 16 …. If you were asked to find the \(100^{th}\) term, what is the general formula you would use to find that value efficiently? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n  = 1 (a_{n-1})^5\)</item>
  <item>\(a_n  = 5+ a_{n-1}\) </item>
  <item>\(a_n = 1 + 5(a_{n-1})\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a_n = 1 +5(n-1)\)</para>
</section>
